        <div id="avoid-fakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Avoid Fake and Tampered Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Counterfeit supplements on Amazon have been a real problem, and one cause was <strong>commingling</strong>: for years, Amazon could pool identical items from different sellers in its warehouses, so the tub you received might come from a different seller than the one you bought from. Amazon announced in 2025 that it would end the practice, and seller-industry reports say the commingled inventory program was scheduled to close at the end of March 2026. That reduces the risk, but it does not remove it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single most useful check is the line under the price that says who the item <strong>ships from</strong> and who it is <strong>sold by</strong>. The safest combinations are sold by Amazon.com, or sold by the brand itself, such as Myprotein North America on the Impact Whey Isolate listing we checked, and shipped by Amazon. A third-party seller you have never heard of, especially at a price far below every other listing, is the clearest warning sign.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the tub arrives, check it before you use it. The safety seal under the lid should be intact, the lot number and best-before date should be printed clearly, and the label should be in English with the US nutrition facts format. If the powder looks, smells or mixes very differently from previous tubs of the same flavor, stop using it and contact the seller and the brand, quoting the lot number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most supplements on Amazon are marked non-returnable, which makes these checks more important. If something is wrong with a third-party order, the A-to-z Guarantee is the route to a refund, and Amazon's report an issue link on the product page is the way to flag a suspected counterfeit. For the wider question of what can go wrong with protein powder itself, our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers contamination and testing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check who it is sold by before you check the price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sold by Amazon.com or by the brand, and shipped by Amazon, is the safest combination. A much cheaper listing from an unknown third-party seller is the one most likely to be old, diverted or fake.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="read-the-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Reading the Listing: Size, Flavor and Packaging
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amazon listings bundle many sizes and flavors under one product page, and the price shown is for whichever variation is selected. Before comparing, select the exact size and flavor you want, then check the price per pound or per ounce Amazon shows beneath the main price. A 5 lb tub that looks expensive next to a 2 lb tub is usually much cheaper per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the nutrition panel image, not just the title. Titles often lead with the most flattering number, such as 30 g of protein, without saying that it takes two scoops. Premier Protein's powder, for example, delivers 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving, which is fine, but you need to know it is two scoops when you compare servings per tub with a product that uses one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Packaging may vary is a phrase you will see on many listings, and it usually means a brand has refreshed its label. It becomes relevant when a formula changes, for example when a brand removes a color additive or changes a sweetener. If an ingredient matters to you, check the panel on the tub that arrives rather than the listing photo, which may show an older version.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the variation reviews filter. Reviews are often pooled across every flavor and size on a listing, so a 4.6-star average may hide a flavor that buyers dislike. Filtering to the flavor you want, and to recent reviews, gives a much better sense of what you will actually receive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscribe-and-save" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Subscribe and Save: Does the Maths Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Subscribe and Save is Amazon's repeat-delivery program. Eligible items usually get a discount of around 5 percent, and on many items that rises to as much as 15 percent when five or more subscriptions arrive in the same monthly delivery, according to Amazon seller guides. The exact percentages are set per listing, so check the product page rather than assuming a number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is what that means for a typical tub. On a $50 protein powder bought once a month, a 5 percent discount saves $2.50 a month, or $30 a year. At 15 percent, the saving is $7.50 a month, or $90 a year, which is close to two free tubs. If you already buy other household staples on subscription, adding your protein powder to the same delivery day can be the easiest way to reach the higher tier.
          </p>

